{:concept/id :framework/core-protocol-boundary
 :concept/type :framework
 :concept/layer :framework-explanation
 :concept/name "Core–protocol ownership boundary"
 :concept/summary
 "The core–protocol ownership boundary distinguishes responsibilities between the generic protocol-independent core, protocol-specific adapters, and concrete accounting layers. It records where abstract policies and artifacts live, where protocol-state translation happens, and where concrete ledger mutation occurs, so that the generic core is not conflated with a protocol-specific implementation."

 :concept/stakeholder-question
 "Which layer owns abstract policies and verification, which owns protocol-state translation, and which owns concrete ledger mutation?"

 :concept/protocols #{:protocol/sew-v1 :protocol/prf}

 :concept/related
 [:framework/held-custody
  :framework/research-command
  :framework/review-certificate
  :concept/conservation]

 :concept/note
  "Ownership boundaries:
   - Generic core owns abstract policies, artifacts, and verification.
   - Protocol-specific adapters own translation between protocol state and generic inputs/effects.
   - Concrete accounting owns protocol-ledger mutation.
   - Receipts bind abstract effects to concrete state changes.
   Concrete-accounting ledger mutation is scope-authorised: the low-level ledger/index write runs only after the applicable scope (position/account partitioning, and force-authorisation status/scope for exceptional reasons) has been validated. The low-level write is an internal implementation detail, not an independent public mutation surface.
   A source-code import prohibition (e.g. generic core must not import a specific protocol) is an architectural direction; it does not by itself prove the generic core is protocol-independent."

 :concept/roles
 {:core-owner {:description "Entity or layer responsible for generic policies, artifacts, and verification."
               :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}
  :adapter-owner {:description "Layer responsible for translating protocol state to/from generic inputs and effects."
                  :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}
  :accounting-owner {:description "Layer responsible for concrete protocol-ledger mutation."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.actor/resolution-module]}}

 :concept/entities
 {:generic-core {:description "Protocol-independent layer owning abstract policies, artifacts, and verification."
                 :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :protocol-adapter {:description "Layer translating protocol-specific state to/from generic inputs and effects."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :concrete-accounting {:description "Layer owning concrete protocol-ledger mutation."
                        :maps-to [:protocol.entity/module]}
  :binding-receipt {:description "Artifact binding an abstract effect to a concrete state change."
                    :maps-to [:protocol.entity/evidence]}}

 :concept/actions
 {:enforce-boundary {:description "Enforce the import/ownership boundary between layers."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.action/check-invariant]}
  :verify-ownership {:description "Verify that each responsibility is held by the correct layer."
                     :maps-to [:protocol.action/verify-allocation]}
  :translate-state {:description "Translate protocol state to/from generic inputs and effects via an adapter."
                    :maps-to [:protocol.action/parameterise]}}

 :concept/outcomes
 {:boundary-respected {:description "Responsibilities are held by the intended layers."
                       :maps-to [:protocol.outcome/released]}
  :boundary-violated {:description "A layer took on a responsibility outside its ownership."
                      :maps-to [:protocol.outcome/adversarial-resolution]}}

 :concept/failure-modes
 [{:failure/id :core-protocol-coupling
   :failure/name "Core–protocol coupling"
   :failure/summary "The generic core depends on a protocol-specific implementation, breaking protocol independence."
   :protocol-mechanisms [:import-boundary :adapter]
   :stakeholder-impact "The generic core can no longer serve other protocols independently."}]

 :concept/assumptions
 ["The generic core owns abstract policies, artifacts, and verification."
  "Protocol-specific adapters own translation between protocol state and generic inputs/effects."
  "Concrete accounting owns protocol-ledger mutation."
  "Receipts bind abstract effects to concrete state changes."
  "An import prohibition is an architectural direction, not proof of protocol independence by itself."
  "Concept labels do not change protocol validity."]

 :concept/out-of-scope
 ["Runtime process isolation."
  "Permission or authorisation enforcement."
  "Deployment-level sandboxing."
  "Absence of semantic coupling through shared data structures."
  "Dependency integrity or package pinning."
  "Protocol-specific behaviour implemented in adapters."
  "External services called dynamically or through configuration."]

 :concept/metrics
 {:adapter-count {:description "Number of protocol adapters bound to the generic core."
                  :source :derived-from-trace}}}
